  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/comms.html#broadcasting-to-other-browsing-contexts\n\nFrom a strict reading of the spec it seems like it should be possible to use `BroadcastChannel` in a unique origin (for example an iframe with `sandbox=\"allow-scripts\"`). And if I understand how unique origins work it should even be possible for such an iframe to create workers (which will share the same unique origin?) and use `BroadcastChannel` to communicate between document and workers.\n\nI'm not sure if that's desired though. For one actually making this work correctly in a unique origin is hard from a chrome implementation point of view (especially for the worker with same unique origin as document case). So maybe the `BroadcastChannel` constructor should just be specified to throw if called from a unique origin? For what it's worth that already seems to be the case in firefox (where trying to create a `BroadcastChannel` instance from a sandboxed iframe throws). Not sure what the most sensible exception would be for this situation though.\n",
